WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 99:59:59.999
Phase 6, AI model types. Different data shapes demand different inductive biases. Convolutions exploit locality, attention models relationships, diffusion models learn to denoise, and graph networks respect structure. Learn what each family is for before reaching for one. This phase has 4 lessons. Lesson 1: Transformers and attention. Transformers use attention to build context-dependent token representations. Queries, keys, and values decide what each position should look at. The question for this lesson: Why can attention models work with long sequences?. Attention lets every token look at every other token and decide who is relevant. In the sentence 'the bank raised its rates', the word bank should lean on raised and rates, not on river. Attention learns that leaning. A transformer stacks attention and feed-forward layers into a deep contextual machine. Positional information tells it where tokens sit, residual connections keep gradients flowing, and multiple heads let it track several relationships at once. You will learn: Attention weights combine information across positions., Multiple heads learn different relationship patterns., Position encoding and residuals make deep stacks trainable.. Practice: Implement one scaled dot-product attention head with small random tensors and verify the output shape before adding a second head.. Watch out for: Forgetting masks and letting future tokens leak in., Ignoring sequence length and attention cost., Treating attention weights as a complete explanation.. You are ready when: Check Q, K, V shapes., Verify the mask., Explain one head.. Recommended video: "Attention in transformers. Lesson 2: Convolutional networks. Convolutions exploit locality and weight sharing. Filters learn local patterns, pooling widens the receptive field, and depth composes them into objects. The question for this lesson: Why do images need a different architecture?. A dense network on pixels throws away the fact that nearby pixels belong together. A convolution slides the same small filter across the image, so a cat detector learned in one corner can fire in another. Channels, stride, padding, and pooling control the shape of the information. Most CNN bugs are shape bugs, and most overfitting is a data or augmentation problem wearing a model costume. You will learn: Weight sharing makes convolutions efficient and translation-aware., The receptive field grows with depth, stride, and pooling., Augmentation must preserve the label you are trying to predict.. Practice: "Train a small CNN on FashionMNIST. Watch out for: Flattening spatial structure too early., Augmenting in a way that changes the label., Comparing models trained with different preprocessing.. You are ready when: Print tensor shapes., Check augmentation examples., Inspect failure cases.. Lesson 3: Diffusion models. Diffusion models learn to reverse a gradual noising process. Training predicts noise; sampling denoises step by step, optionally guided by text. The question for this lesson: How do generative models turn noise into an image?. The forward process destroys an image with Gaussian noise. The model learns the reverse direction: given a noisy sample and a timestep, predict the noise that was added. Sampling starts from pure noise and applies that learned denoiser repeatedly. Latent diffusion runs the process in a compressed space, which makes high-resolution generation practical. Steps, guidance scale, and the base model change both quality and cost, so compare them under a fixed prompt set. You will learn: Training adds noise; sampling removes it in learned steps., Latent diffusion trades pixel detail for compute efficiency., Guidance and step count change quality, diversity, and latency.. Practice: "Run the same prompt at several step counts and guidance values. Record quality. Watch out for: Comparing outputs generated with different settings., Treating a generated image as evidence of a real event., Ignoring model licenses and provenance.. You are ready when: Fix the prompt set., Record settings., Check license and provenance.. Lesson 4: Graph neural networks. Graph networks pass messages along edges. Node, edge, and graph tasks need different readouts, and graph structure creates leakage risks. The question for this lesson: How do you learn from relationships instead of rows?. Some data is not a table; it is a network. Users connect to users, molecules have bonds, and documents cite each other. A graph neural network updates each node by aggregating information from its neighbors. Stack too many message-passing layers and every node starts to look the same. Split edges by time or entity, and make sure validation graphs do not share the exact edges you are trying to predict. You will learn: Message passing aggregates neighbor features., Readout differs for node, edge, and whole-graph prediction., Deep GNNs can oversmooth; more layers are not always better.. Practice: "Build a small citation or social graph. Watch out for: Leaking future edges into training., Shuffling nodes but keeping the same edges across splits., Ignoring the oversmoothing plateau.. You are ready when: Define the prediction unit., Split by graph structure., Compare to a feature baseline.. A mental map from data shape and task to a sensible model family. Take your time with each lesson and practice the examples.
